1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Before humans had words, they had something else. And whatever that was, it kept us alive, coordinated, and somehow managed to build the foundation

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:13,000
of every language spoken today. But here's the thing nobody really asks.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:20,000
What exactly were we doing before speech? Like, what did communication look like when the best tool you had was your face and a grunt? How did early

4
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:30,000
humans say, "Hey, there's a lion behind you." without actually saying it. That's what we're getting into today. And the answer is stranger, richer, and more impressive than most people assume.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:39,000
Let's start with something you already know how to do, but probably don't think about right now. Without moving your mouth, you can tell someone you're annoyed. You can signal that you're

6
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:47,000
confused, suspicious, delighted, or deeply unimpressed. All without producing a single syllable. Your eyebrows do it. Your posture does it.

7
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:54,000
The slight tightening around your eyes does it. This is not a coincidence. This is ancient hardware. The human face has

8
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:02,000
43 muscles. That's more than almost any other animal on the planet. And the overwhelming majority of them exist not for chewing or breathing, but for

9
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:10,000
expression. Evolution built us a communication device out of meat and bone. And it did it long before language showed up. Researchers who study facial

10
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:19,000
expressions across cultures have found something striking. The basic expressions are universal. Fear looks the same in rural Papua New Guinea as it

11
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:25,000
does in downtown Tokyo. Disgust is disgust whether you're in the Amazon rainforest or a Norwegian suburb.

12
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:29,000
Happiness, anger, sadness, surprise.

13
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:36,000
These aren't learned behaviors. They're biological broadcasts. Paul Ecman, a psychologist who spent decades mapping

14
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:43,000
the human face, identified what he called micro expressions, involuntary flickers of emotion that last less than

15
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:49,000
a fifth of a second. You can't fully control them. They leak through even when you're trying to hide how you feel.

16
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:58,000
These aren't cultural artifacts. They're evolutionary ones. Meaning, our ancestors were already transmitting and receiving emotional data this way

17
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:06,000
millions of years before anyone said a word. And this matters because facial expression isn't passive. It's not just a side effect of feeling something. It's

18
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:14,000
a signal shaped by natural selection because it helped coordinate group behavior. An individual who could instantly broadcast danger across a camp

19
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:22,000
without making noise that attracted the predator had a survival advantage. So did the one who could correctly read that signal and react in half a second.

20
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:28,000
We didn't invent communication with language. We upgraded it. Now let's go back way back before homo sapiens.

21
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:36,000
Before even our immediate ancestors about 6 million years ago, the lineage that would eventually produce humans split from the one that would produce

22
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:44,000
chimpanzees. And if you watch chimpanzees today, you'll see something that looks uncomfortably familiar. They gesture deliberately with clear intent.

23
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:53,000
A chimp will extend an arm, palm up in a begging gesture. They'll touch another chimp's shoulder to initiate grooming. They'll wave an arm to solicit play.

24
00:02:53,000 --> 00:03:00,000
They'll use a specific gesture, a kind of beckoning motion, to invite another individual to follow. And critically, they only use these gestures when they

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:09,000
can see the other chimp's face. They wait for eye contact. They repeat a gesture if ignored. They stop when they get a response. This isn't instinct

26
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:18,000
running on autopilot. This is intentional communication. Researchers at the University of St. Andrews cataloged over 80 distinct gestures used

27
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:26,000
by wild chimpanzees and they found that a significant portion of them carried consistent meaning across different groups. Even groups that had never been

28
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:35,000
in contact with each other which tells you these gestures are old, older than the populations using them, possibly older than the species itself. Bonobos

29
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:42,000
do this too. Gorillas, orangutans. The gestural system in great apes is remarkably sophisticated, and it

30
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:50,000
strongly suggests that before our ancestors ever made a word, they were already operating with a functional system of intentional signals. Here's

31
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:59,000
the twist. Human infants communicate through gesture before they speak. At around 9 months, before any real language emerges, babies start pointing.

32
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:03,000
They hold up objects to show you things. They reach towards something they want.

33
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:11,000
They wave. These gestures follow a very specific developmental sequence and it mirrors what we see in other great apes.

34
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:18,000
Pointing in particular is interesting because most animals can't do it. You can spend your whole life with a dog and

35
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:29,000
it will look at your finger, not what you're pointing at. Human infants point to share attention, not just to request things. That distinction is significant.

36
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:37,000
The gestural origin theory of language, one of the leading hypotheses in linguistics, argues that language didn't start in the mouth. It started in the

37
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:44,000
hands and the voice came later as an addition to a system that already existed. There's actually a neurological

38
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:52,000
argument for this, too. The brain region most associated with language production, Braa's area, is located in

39
00:04:52,000 --> 00:05:01,000
the same general territory that controls fine hand and mouth movements. It's not a language region that happens to sit near the motor cortex. It may be a motor

40
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:08,000
planning region that got conscripted into language. The hands built the road. The mouth just learned to drive on it.

41
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:17,000
But gesture only works when you can see each other. And early humans spent a lot of time in situations where visibility was limited. Moving through dense

42
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:24,000
forest, operating in darkness, spread out across terrain where line of sight wasn't guaranteed. So something else had

43
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:33,000
to fill the gap. Sound, not language, not words, just sound. Before words, there were calls, vocalizations that

44
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:42,000
carried meaning not through grammar or vocabulary, but through acoustic properties. The pitch, the rhythm, the intensity, the pattern of the sound.

45
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:50,000
Every primate species has a call system, and these systems can be surprisingly specific. Vervet monkeys famously have

46
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:58,000
distinct alarm calls for different types of predators. One call for eagles, which sends the troops scrambling into the bushes. A different call for snakes,

47
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:05,000
which makes them stand up and scan the ground. Another for leopards, which sends them into the trees. Same species,

48
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:11,000
same troop, three different sounds, three different behavioral responses.

49
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:20,000
Context specific, consistent, and functionally referential. Meaning the call isn't just expressing an emotional state. It's referring to something out

50
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:27,000
in the world. This is not language. But it's not nothing either. It sits somewhere in the middle. A system of

51
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:35,000
signals that carries information beyond what the raw emotion of the caller would convey. Our ancestors almost certainly had something like this. Probably richer

52
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:43,000
than what vervitz have. Given that our lineage had millions of years of social complexity stacking up on top of it. And as group sizes grew, as social

53
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:51,000
hierarchies got more complicated, as cooperative hunting required more coordination, the selective pressure to communicate more precisely would have

54
00:06:51,000 --> 00:07:00,000
intensified. What early homo would have sounded like is genuinely unknown. The voice box doesn't fossilize. The soft

55
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:07,000
tissue that produces speech leaves no trace in the archaeological record, but we can make inferences from anatomy. The

56
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:14,000
position of the hyoid bone, a small U-shaped bone in the throat, tells us something about vocal anatomy.

57
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:23,000
Neanderthalss had a hyoid bone nearly identical to ours, which means they almost certainly had the physical capacity for complex vocalizations.

58
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:31,000
Whether those vocalizations were language in any meaningful sense is still debated, but the hardware was there. And here's the thing about

59
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:40,000
Neanderthalss that most people don't sit with long enough. They buried their dead. They made pigments. There's evidence they wore eagle talons as

60
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:48,000
personal ornaments. These are not the behaviors of creatures with nothing to say to each other. These are the behaviors of creatures navigating a rich

61
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:55,000
social world that almost certainly required some form of symbolic communication to maintain. Let's talk about something that doesn't get enough

62
00:07:55,000 --> 00:08:02,000
credit. Rhythm. Before words, there was beat. Rhythm is one of the most ancient and cross-cultural features of human

63
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:10,000
communication. Every known human culture has music. Every known human culture has drumming, clapping, or some form of

64
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:18,000
rhythmic expression. This is not coincidence. Rhythm is deeply embedded in social bonding in a way that's hard to explain without thinking about

65
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:26,000
evolution. When humans synchronize movement, when they walk together, drum together, sing together, something

66
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:38,000
neurological happens. Oxytocin levels increase. Pain thresholds rise. Group cohesion measurably improves. There's a reason military units march in step.

67
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:46,000
There's a reason religious rituals involve chanting. There's a reason concerts feel the way they do. Shared rhythm signals affiliation. It says we

68
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:54,000
are together. We are moving as one. We can be trusted. This didn't require language. And given that the capacity

69
00:08:54,000 --> 00:09:02,000
for rhythmic entrainment, the ability to synchronize with an external beat is something humans do automatically and

70
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:09,000
other primates largely can't. It suggests this was a significant and specific adaptation in our lineage.

71
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:18,000
Anthropologist Robin Dunar, who's known for the social brain hypothesis, has argued that early music and rhythmic vocalization might have functioned as a

