00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:26,000
kind of grooming at a distance. In primate groups, physical grooming is the primary social bonding mechanism. But

73
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:33,000
grooming is one-on-one. As group sizes expanded, you couldn't groom your way to social cohesion in a group of 150

74
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:44,000
people. You needed something that could bond many individuals simultaneously, singing together, moving together, drumming together, sound as social glue.

75
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:52,000
This isn't a fringe idea anymore. It's supported by evidence from neuroscience, pimeatlogy, and anthropology.

76
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:59,000
The communicative function of rhythm in early humans may have been as important as anything gestural or vocal in a more

77
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:06,000
referential sense. The oldest known musical instruments, bone flutes, found in sites across Europe, date to at least

78
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:13,000
40,000 years ago. But the absence of older instruments doesn't mean there was no older music. Percussion doesn't need

79
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:21,000
instruments. hands on hollow logs, stones struck together, feet on hard ground. Rhythm was available to every

80
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:29,000
human who ever lived. And the neurological response to it suggests we've been using it for far longer than 40,000 years. Now, let's talk about the

81
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:38,000
body. Posture communicates. It has for as long as there have been bodies to adopt it. Making yourself large when

82
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:45,000
threatened. Making yourself small when deferring to a dominant individual, presenting a shoulder, turning away,

83
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:54,000
dropping the head. These are not symbolic behaviors. They're biologically readable. Other animals read them accurately. We read them accurately.

84
00:10:54,000 --> 00:11:01,000
Early humans operating in complex social groups would have used postural and proxmic cues, the study of how space is

85
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:09,000
used in communication, constantly and fluently. Who stands close to whom? Who turns their body toward whom during a

86
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:17,000
confrontation? Who takes up space at the center of a group and who shrinks toward the edges. These signals establish

87
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:25,000
hierarchy, signal alliance, communicate threat and submission without a single vocalization.

88
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:33,000
Touch matters here too. Tactile communication, who is allowed to touch whom, where, in what context, is one of

89
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:44,000
the most primitive and powerful channels of social information. A hand on the shoulder reads differently from a hand on the head. A quick tap reads differently from a sustained grip.

90
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:53,000
Across cultures and even across species, the semantics of touch are remarkably consistent because they're pre-linguistic. They're older than

91
00:11:53,000 --> 00:12:00,000
words. All of this gesture, posture, touch, space is what researchers today

92
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:07,000
call non-verbal communication. But that term is a bit misleading because it frames these systems as secondary to

93
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:14,000
verbal communication, as if they're just the leftover stuff that couldn't make it into words. In reality, these systems

94
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:22,000
are primary. They came first. They're more reliable in certain contexts, harder to fake in certain ways, and they

95
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:29,000
still carry enormous informationational load, even among fully language fluent adults. Studies consistently show that

96
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:38,000
when verbal and non-verbal signals contradict each other, people trust the non-verbal. Every time a person says, "I'm fine," in a flat voice with their

97
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:47,000
arms crossed and their eyes somewhere else. You don't believe the words. You believe the body. That's not irrationality. That's an ancient system

98
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:53,000
correctly overwriting a newer and more easily manipulated one. We didn't stop using the system when we got language.

99
00:12:53,000 --> 00:13:01,000
We layered language on top of it. And the two systems still run simultaneously every time two humans interact. Here's something genuinely interesting that

100
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:10,000
tends to get overlooked in this conversation. Smell. Humans are often described as having a poor sense of smell compared to other mammals. And in

101
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:17,000
absolute terms, yes, your dog is living in an oldactory universe you can barely imagine. But that framing misses

102
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:29,000
something. Humans are extraordinarily sensitive to specific chemo signals that carry social information. We can smell fear, not metaphorically, literally.

103
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:37,000
Studies have shown that compounds in the sweat of people who are genuinely frightened trigger measurable changes in the physiological and neural states of

104
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:47,000
people who smell them. Even when those people consciously believe they're just smelling a neutral sweat sample, the signal bypasses conscious processing and go straight to threat response systems.

105
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:55,000
We can smell stress. We can smell disease. People with certain illnesses emit detectable chemical signatures that healthy individuals can under some

106
00:13:55,000 --> 00:14:02,000
conditions detect. We can smell genetic compatibility. The famous sweaty t-shirt studies showed that people consistently

107
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:11,000
rate the smell of individuals with sufficiently different immune system profiles as more attractive, which is evolution quietly doing mate selection calculations without telling the

108
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:18,000
conscious brain. Early humans in close physical proximity, sleeping in the same space, moving in tight groups, handling

109
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:24,000
the same materials, would have been bathed in olfactory social information continuously.

110
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:32,000
We can't reconstruct exactly how much of this was consciously processed versus operating below the threshold of awareness. But the fact that these

111
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:39,000
cheosensory systems are still active and still influencing human behavior today in an era of deodorant and social

112
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:50,000
distancing suggests they were substantial. Smell doesn't work as a precise messaging system. You can't smell a question or smell an argument.

113
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:58,000
But as a broadcast of emotional state, physiological status, and genetic information, it was likely a constant

114
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:06,000
background channel of social communication that early humans navigated fluently. Let's get to something that feels more concrete.

115
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:14,000
Proto language. At some point, the gap between what our ancestors were doing, calls, gesture, rhythm, expression, and

116
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:22,000
what we'd recognize as language began to close. The question is when and how. and what the intermediate stages looked

117
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:29,000
like. The honest answer is that we don't know exactly. Language leaves no fossils. What we have are proxy

118
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:37,000
indicators. Skull morphology suggesting brain organization, hyoid bones suggesting vocal anatomy, and crucially

119
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:48,000
stone tools. The toolmaking traditions of early homo are, in a strange way, a window into cognitive and possibly communicative capacity.

120
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:58,000
The old tools, the earliest recognizable stone tools going back about 2.6 million years, are relatively simple. A stone

121
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:06,000
napped with deliberate strikes to create a sharp edge. Not complicated. The Akalan hand axe, which

122
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:16,000
appears around 1.76 million years ago, is different in a meaningful way. It requires planning. You have to hold in mind the shape you're working toward.

123
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:27,000
make decisions multiple steps ahead of the current action and execute a sequence of precise movements toward a non-immed immediately visible goal. This is hierarchical sequential cognition.

124
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:36,000
The same cognitive architecture that underlies syntax, the same architecture you use when constructing a sentence where each word choice constrains and is

