00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:57,000
communication system to that same system, plus grammar. And once grammar arrived, the ability to combine a finite set of signals into an infinite set of meanings, everything accelerated.

134
00:17:57,000 --> 00:18:00,000
There's one more thing worth discussing.

135
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:08,000
The role of teaching. Communication isn't just about transmitting information in the moment. One of its most powerful functions is transmitting

136
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:17,000
information across time from one generation to the next. And the evidence suggests that early humans were doing this long before language made it easy.

137
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:25,000
Imitation is one mechanism. Watching a skilled individual perform a complex task and copying it, refining it,

138
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:32,000
improving it over generations is a form of cultural transmission that doesn't require words. And humans are

139
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:40,000
extraordinarily good at it. Imitation in humans is qualitatively different from what other animals do. We imitate the

140
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:47,000
method, not just the outcome. We reproduce the specific actions used to achieve a result even when other methods

141
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:54,000
would also work. This is called overimitation and it's so deeply wired into us that children will reproduce

142
00:18:54,000 --> 00:19:02,000
obviously unnecessary steps in a demonstrated action sequence because they assume the steps must mean something. This behavioral tendency is

143
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:10,000
what allows culture to accumulate. A technique developed by one individual can be preserved and refined by a group and transmitted to the next generation

144
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:19,000
without full verbal explanation. The form carries the information. You watch, you copy, you improve, you pass it on.

145
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:27,000
Scaffolded demonstration, where a skilled individual guides a learner through a process, pointing, redirecting, repeating, would have

146
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:36,000
worked as a teaching system before language. It still works this way in many contexts today. Watch a good sushi chef teach an apprentice. Watch a

147
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:45,000
basketball player teach a crossover dribble. A tremendous amount of the instruction is physical, demonstrative, non-verbal. Language assists, but it

148
00:19:45,000 --> 00:19:49,000
doesn't do the whole job. There's something almost poetic about this.

149
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:58,000
Early human knowledge. How to nap a stone, how to read animal tracks, which plants were medicine and which were death. Survived for hundreds of

150
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:05,000
thousands of years before anyone could write it down or even fully articulate it. It survived in bodies, in hands that

151
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:14,000
had watched other hands and learned to move the same way, in the accumulated muscle memory of a species that was teaching long before it had teachers.

152
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:22,000
The emergence of more explicit symbolic communication, proto language, then language, would have dramatically

153
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:29,000
increased the efficiency and fidelity of this knowledge transfer. You could describe things that weren't present.

154
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:36,000
You could communicate about the past and the future. You could explain the why behind a technique, not just demonstrate

155
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:45,000
the how. You could warn someone about a danger they hadn't encountered yet. You could grieve together for someone who was gone. You could make plans that

156
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:52,000
stretched further than the horizon. This is probably a large part of why language spread and deepened once it began. Not

157
00:20:52,000 --> 00:21:00,000
just because it helped in the moment, but because it supercharged the cultural accumulation that makes human civilization possible. Here's where it

158
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:08,000
all lands. Human communication before speech wasn't primitive. It wasn't a rough draft. It was a sophisticated

159
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:16,000
multi-channel system that coordinated groups of dozens or hundreds of individuals, managed complex social hierarchies, transmitted emotional

160
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:24,000
states and threat information in real time, and built the cultural scaffolding across which language eventually emerged. The face was already

161
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:32,000
broadcasting. The hands were already signaling. The voice was already producing calls with referential meaning. The body was already carrying

162
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:40,000
status and alliance information. Smell was already filtering social data below the level of conscious awareness. Rhythm

163
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:49,000
was already bonding groups together in ways that no one-on-one interaction could achieve. Language didn't replace this system. It joined it. Every

164
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:56,000
conversation you have today runs simultaneously on ancient pre-linguistic channels. the expression on your face,

165
00:21:56,000 --> 00:22:05,000
the posture of your body, the rhythm of your speech, the touch that is or isn't offered, and on the more recently evolved channel of words and grammar.

166
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:12,000
When you look at a stranger and in a fraction of a second decide whether they seem trustworthy, that's the old system

