00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:45,000
before their conscious brain had finished processing the sentence. We are not creatures who traded in non-verbal communication for language. We are

171
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:54,000
creatures who added language to a non-verbal foundation, and we still run on both. And when you zoom out far enough, the whole story becomes

172
00:22:54,000 --> 00:23:01,000
something worth sitting with. A lineage of animals spread across millions of years, gradually building a more and

173
00:23:01,000 --> 00:23:06,000
more complex system for reaching across the space between one mind and another.

174
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:17,000
Starting with a face that could broadcast fear. Adding hands that could point and gesture. Adding voices that could carry information over distance.

175
00:23:17,000 --> 00:23:26,000
Adding rhythm that could pull individuals into synchrony. Adding imitation that could carry knowledge across generations. And then eventually

176
00:23:26,000 --> 00:23:35,000
adding the thing that let one mind describe the contents of another mind to someone who wasn't there, wasn't present, didn't see it happen. the thing

177
00:23:35,000 --> 00:23:43,000
we call language. Which means every time you say something without words, every shrug, every look, every deliberate

178
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:52,000
silence, you're not failing to communicate. You're reaching back to the oldest version of what you are. You're speaking in a language so ancient it

179
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:55,000
doesn't have a name, one that was refined across millions of years of survival, cooperation, and everything in between. And somehow most of the time the message gets through just fine.
